      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(1+2x)^{4},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=1+2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (2) \\\\  &=8(1+2x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=0} &=[8(1+2x)^{3}]|_{x=0} \\\\  &=8(1+2(0))^{3} \\\\  &=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,1)$ is $m=8.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1) &=8(x-(0)) \\\\ y &=8 x+1 \\\\ y &=8 x + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
